
Q1:Rate limiter check ka acceptable overhead kitna hai — overall request latency me? 

Specifically:
- Normal load pe kitna? — jaise p99 < 5ms?
- Peak load pe thoda degrade chalega? — jaise p99 < 20ms?
- Ya business side se koi hard SLA defined hai?

Aur do edge cases bhi clarify karna chahta hoon:

- Peak load pe — connection pool wait time badh sakta hai, to latency thodi badhegi. Acceptable hai?
- Redis (counter store) down ho jaye — us failure mode me kya latency expectation hai? Fail-closed me near-zero hogi,
  lekin fail-open me bhi ensure karna padega ki Redis connect attempt pe 500ms waste na ho — iske liye Circuit Breaker lagega."


Apne smaajh ke liye


Rate limiting mai multiple reasons se latency aa sakti hai like :

Source 1: Rule Fetching Latency

Agar dynamic config hai, to rules 3-layer architecture (Local Cache → Redis → DB) se fetch honge:

Layer	Latency	              Kab Hit Hoga
Local Cache	                ~0ms (nanoseconds)	99% requests (hot path)
Redis	1-3ms	                TTL expire hone pe (cold path)
DB	10-50ms	                Redis miss pe (rare)

Key insight: Ye latency har request pe nahi lagti. Sirf TTL expire hone pe lagti hai.
Matlab 60 sec me ek baar. Average latency me ye negligible ho jaati hai.


Source 2: Counter Operation Latency (Redis Call)

Har request pe Redis call karni padegi — INCR / GET — counter check aur update ke liye.
Ye latency unavoidable hai distributed setup me, kyunki Multiple application instances hote hain
and sabko same shared counter chahiye. Agar har instance apna local counter rakhe, to total limit
cross ho jayegi (rate limit inconsistency)

Isliye: Rate limiter check ki latency ek Redis round-trip (~1–3ms) se kam nahi ho sakti. 
Ye teri minimum latency floor hai.

### NOTE:
----------

Distributed rate limiter me Redis round-trip latency minimum 1-3ms hoti hai — ye avoidable nahi hai. 
Kyunki shared counter Redis me hi rahega (sabhi instances same counter read/update karenge).
Isliye < 2ms hard constraint practically possible nahi hai.
Agar latency itni strict chahiye, to local counter use karna padega — har instance apna counter apni
local memory me rakhega. Isse latency ~0ms ho jayegi, lekin trade-off hai: instances ke beech exact sync 
nahi hoga, isliye rate limiting approximate ho jayegi — kuch instances over-allow karenge
, kuch under-allow. Matlab consistency lose ho jayegi.


Source 3: Connection Pool Wait Time

- Manlo 200 application threads hain, lekin Redis connection pool sirf 50 connections ka hai.
- Agar 50 application threads already Redis call kar rahe hain, to 51st application thread wait karega
  connection available hone ka.
- Ye wait time bhi latency me add hota hai. Peak load pe ye 5-20ms tak ja sakta hai.
- Solution: Connection (Lettuce/Jedis), pool size tune karna.

 
Source 4: Serialization/Deserialization

- Rule DB se fetch hua — JSON format me hai. Usse Java object me convert karna padta hai (deserialize).
- Rule update karte waqt - Java object → JSON banana → DB me store (Serialize)
- Ye chhota hai (~0.1-0.5ms), lekin count karna zaroori hai.


Source 4: Rate limiter fail hone par bhi latency introduce ho sakti hai 

- Agar humne FR me fail-closed approach decide kiya tha, to Redis down hone par request immediately reject karni hai — us case me latency near-zero hogi. 

- Lekin agar fail-open approach decide kiya tha, to hume ye ensure karna hoga ki latency almost zero rahe. 
   Ye automatically nahi hoga — iske liye fail-fast implementation zaroori hai.

- Agar bad design kiya (fail-open ke saath), to latency introduce ho sakti hai. Jaise maan lo application ne Redis ko request bheji TCP connection 
  establish karne ki koshish ki — aur humne connection timeout 500ms configure kar diya. Ab Redis down hai, to application thread wait karega us
  configured connection timeout tak, uske baad pata chalega ki Redis down hai. Ab is case me, although humne fail-open approach decide kiya tha, 
  phir bhi 500ms per request ki latency introduce ho gayi — kyunki har request pe Redis connect karne ki koshish ho rahi hai, 
  timeout ka wait ho raha hai, phir decision aa raha hai.

- Isse bachne ke liye hume Circuit Breaker pattern use karna hoga. Circuit Breaker jaise hi Redis ko down detect karta hai, 
  wo Redis calls ko band kar deta hai — bina Redis connect karne ki koshish kiye, immediately fail-open decision leta hai aur
  request directly target system ko allow kar deta hai. Isse failure mode me bhi total latency ~0ms rahegi.




Q2: Kitne concurrent users expect kar rahe hain peak pe ? Peak pe kitni RPS aayegi ?

Khudke samajh ke liye

Concurrent Users kya hai?

- Wo users jo ek specific moment pe system se actively connected hain — unka session active hai aur wo requests bhej sakte hain.
- Example:
   10M registered users
   but only 1M daily active users → Concurrent users (peak) = 100K


RPS (Requests Per Second) kya hai?

- Ek second me system pe kitni HTTP requests aa rahi hain — actual incoming traffic.
- RPS = Concurrent Users × Average Requests per User per Second
- Example:
  Same concurrent users, different RPS possible:
  => 100K concurrent users, har user 1 req/min → 1,666 RPS
  => 100K concurrent users, har user 10 req/sec → 1M RPS
  => 100K concurrent users, har user 0.1 req/sec → 10K RPS

TRAP: Concurrent users se RPS directly nahi nikalta — average requests per user per second chahiye.

RPS se pata chalega Redis Capacity  i.e single Redis chalega ya redis cluster chahiye request counter ke liye .

NOTE:
Hum do alag Redis ki baat kar rahe hain. agar hume dymaic configuration opt kiya hai rate limitng rule ske liye  to us e cas emai hum 3 layer architecture 
use karenge so us wale Rules wale Redis pe load bahut kam hai becasue  rules rarely change hote hain, Isliye single node bhi chalega.
Counter Redis pe har request hit karti hai — ye critical hai, iska load calculate karenge.


RPS se Redis Capacity — Kaise Decide Karein

Decision Formula

Single Redis chalega ya Cluster?

IF RPS <= 50,000  → Single Redis chalega ✅ 
IF RPS > 50,000   → Cluster chahiye ❌

Note: Counter pe minimum 1 GET + 1 UPDATE operation per request hota hai. Lekin ye dono operations mostly Lua script ke andar hote hain
(race condition avoid karne ke liye). Isliye capacity calculation me 1 Lua script call = 1 Redis operation maan lo.

Single Redis node capacity:
- Conservative: 50K ops/sec (safe)
- Actual: 80K-100K ops/sec tak handle kar sakta hai

Case-wise Design :

Case 1: Interviewer bole "10K RPS"

10,000 RPS < 50,000 → Single Redis chalega ✅

Design:
- Single Redis master
- 1 replica (HA ke liye — crash ho to promote ho jaye)
- Total: 2 Redis nodes


Case 2: Interviewer bole "100K RPS"

100,000 RPS > 50,000 → Cluster chahiye 

Design:
- Kitne nodes? = 100K / 50K = 2 nodes
- Headroom ke saath = 3 nodes (safe side)
- Each master + 1 replica
- Total: 6 Redis nodes (3 master + 3 replica)


Case 3: Interviewer bole "1M RPS"

1,000,000 RPS > 50,000 → Cluster chahiye 

Design:
- Kitne nodes? = 1M / 50K = 20 nodes
- Headroom ke saath = 25 nodes (safe side)
- Each master + 2 replicas
- Total: 75 Redis nodes (25 master + 50 replica)

